# API and data

## Routes

- Validate with Zod schemas from `packages/shared`; never hand-parse a body.
- Authorize with `can()` before touching data.
- Domain writes and sync apply share one code path, so `ChangeLog` stays consistent. Never write a domain table without emitting its change.
- Sync apply must be idempotent — replaying the same push changes nothing.

## Drizzle

- Postgres dialect only (`pg-core`). Do not add a second dialect or a server-side SQLite path.
- The schema file is the source of truth; generate migrations with drizzle-kit and never hand-edit one that has already shipped.

## Migrations must not destroy user data

Self-hosters run these unattended, so a bad migration is unrecoverable data loss.

- Additive by default: add a nullable column, backfill, then tighten in a later release.
- Never drop or rename a column or table, and never change a type destructively, in a single shipped migration. Deprecate first, remove once no client writes it.
- Deletes are soft (`deleted_at`) because tombstones drive sync.

```sql
-- ❌ silently loses data on every existing instance
ALTER TABLE points DROP COLUMN description;

-- ✅ deprecate now, remove in a later release
ALTER TABLE points ALTER COLUMN description DROP NOT NULL;
```

## Operational

Migrations run at startup behind a lock. The API stays a single container with in-process WebSocket fan-out — do not add code that assumes multiple replicas.
